== Part F |~Buyers and Preferences

_Buyers decisions make up the demand curve._

This part aims to develop the model behind the demand curve.

We're going to start by thinking about how the individual chooses quantity. This is demand, which is the result of the budget constraint and preferences represented by the indifference space. And given some quantity, which buyers have no direct control over, prices happen according to Edgeworth. It makes everyone happy given their endowment.

Construct the individual demand curve. Then the demand curve.

This is where we do the demand simulation, keeping in the background all the students' alternative uses of their money.

=== Episode F1 | Consumer Choice

Given their budget constraint from factor markets, consumers maximize utility by choosing the optimal combination of goods where marginal utility per dollar is equal across all goods.

PT1. Utility.~This video introduces the utility as a way of turning preferences into numbers with no intrisic zero.

PT2. Budget Constraint. This video introduces the budget constraint as a way to use prices to represent the inherent scarcity we face.

=== Episode F2 | Demand

This video introduces the individual's constrained optimization decision intuitively and how it gives us demand.

=== Episode F3 | Labor Markets

Labor markets determine the income that households earn from selling their labor, which becomes the budget constraint for consumer choice.

=== Episode F5 | Monopsony

This video builds a model of monopsony.

=== Episode F6. Edgeworth Box

This video introduces the Edgeworth box as a way to talk about how prices allow us to distribute the resources we have available to us. Then tie back to the PPF.
